course: dictionary
lesson: 636
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words can be a thing and an action. Backpack is one of them. A backpack is a thing: a bag with two straps that you wear on your back. To backpack is an action: travelling and hiking with everything you need in a big backpack.
explain: The words around the word show you which one is meant. «She packed her backpack for school.» Packed and school tell you it is the bag. «They went backpacking through the mountains for a month.» Went and mountains tell you it is the action.
explain: Check your choice by trying a meaning in the sentence. Does "a bag with two straps" fit? Or does "travel carrying everything you need" fit? The one that makes sense is the right one.
fact: A noun names a thing, and a verb shows an action.
fact: «Backpack» as a noun means a bag with two straps that you carry on your back.
fact: «Backpack» as a verb means to travel or hike carrying everything you need in a big backpack.
fact: A backpack is also called a schoolbag or rucksack.
fact: Wearing both straps spreads the weight across your back and shoulders, which is kinder to your back.
exercise: «She zipped her backpack shut and ran for the bus.» In this sentence, what is a backpack? | To travel far carrying everything you need | *A bag with two straps | A kind of bus ticket
exercise: «They plan to backpack across Spain next summer.» What does backpack mean here? | A bag with two straps | To pitch a tent by the sea | To lose something on a trip | *To travel carrying everything you need on your back
exercise: «Put your reading book in your backpack.» Is backpack a noun or a verb in this sentence? | *Noun, a thing | Adverb, describing how you put it | Verb, an action | Adjective, describing a book
test: «Remember to wear both straps on your backpack.» What does backpack mean here? | To travel far on foot | To lose a strap | *A bag with two straps | A kind of lunchbox
test: «Lots of students backpack around Europe after they finish school.» What does backpack mean here? | To wear a coat in the rain | *To travel carrying everything you need | A bag with two straps | To hide a bag under a bench
exam: Which sentence uses backpack as a verb? | «The backpack has two straps.» | *«They will backpack through Peru all summer.» | «He lost his backpack on the bus.» | «Her backpack is bright red.»
